direction: down

title: |md
  ### L2 → L3 demux: scoped ptype lists, pt_prev, and list delivery
| {near: top-center; shape: text}

reg: "Registration: dev_add_pack" {
  style.fill: "#ede9fe"
  reg_body: |md
    `ptype_head()` selects a list:

    Built-in L3 handler (ETH_P_IP)
      → `ptype_base[type & 0xF]`

    AF_PACKET ETH_P_ALL tap
      → netns/device `ptype_all`

    Scoped protocol-bound socket
      → netns/device `ptype_specific`

    IPv4 registers:
    `.func = ip_rcv`
    `.list_func = ip_list_rcv`
  |
}

classify: "__netif_receive_skb_core" {
  style.fill: "#dbeafe"
  c_body: |md
    `skb->protocol` came from eth_type_trans.

    1. Visit netns/device `ptype_all` taps.
    2. Run ingress, VLAN, RX-handler logic;
       `skb->dev` may change.
    3. Walk global ethertype bucket.
    4. Walk applicable netns/original/current-
       device `ptype_specific` lists.

    Exact list selection is scope-aware;
    it is not one global three-list sequence.
  |
}

defer: "pt_prev defers the final consumer" {
  style.fill: "#fef3c7"
  d_body: |md
    Each matching list entry becomes `pt_prev`.

    If another match appears, flush the earlier
    one via `deliver_skb`:
      increment `skb->users`
      call earlier `.func`

    One final handler needs no extra reference.
    For scalar input, `INDIRECT_CALL_INET`
    promotes ip_rcv / ipv6_rcv.
  |
}

listpath: "Batched receive keeps a list" {
  style.fill: "#dcfce7"
  l_body: |md
    `__netif_receive_skb_list_core` classifies
    each skb with the same core, then groups
    adjacent packets by final `packet_type`
    and original device.

    Handler has `.list_func`?
      → one batched call (IPv4: ip_list_rcv)

    Otherwise
      → fall back to per-skb `.func` calls
  |
}

fanout: "What a capture tap changes" {
  style.fill: "#fff7ed"
  f_body: |md
    Without a tap: ip_rcv can remain the final
    consumer and take the original reference.

    With an ETH_P_ALL capture tap: that earlier
    consumer must be flushed before the later
    L3 handler. No deep payload copy is required,
    but fan-out and refcount work are real.
  |
}

drop: "No matching handler" {
  style.fill: "#fee2e2"
  drop_body: |md
    `pt_prev == NULL`

    increment RX dropped for unhandled protocol
    release skb with a drop reason
  |
}

reg -> classify: "lists are ready before traffic"
classify -> defer: "matches share one deferred pointer"
defer -> listpath: "list input groups final matches"
defer -> fanout: "second consumer flushes first"
classify -> drop: "no match" {style.stroke-dash: 4}
